   rc = ldap_win_bind(data, server, user, passwd);Description: Fix CVE-2026-8924 -- cookie "super-cookie" PSL bypass via trailing-dot Domain
 curl's cookie parser delegates public-suffix acceptability to libpsl via
 psl_is_cookie_domain_acceptable(). Because the raw request host and the raw
 Set-Cookie "Domain" attribute were passed to libpsl without stripping the
 optional trailing FQDN "." (RFC 1034 root indicator), a hostile origin served
 from "foo.co.uk." returning "Set-Cookie: n=v; Domain=co.uk." bypassed the
 check: libpsl compares byte-for-byte, so "co.uk." does not match the "co.uk"
 public-suffix entry, acceptable == true, and curl stored a cross-domain
 "super-cookie" that was then attached to any subsequent request whose host
 matched the trailing-dot suffix -- a cross-site cookie-scoping bypass /
 information-disclosure primitive (CWE-201).
 .
 Backported from upstream commit 51beed175dbfc37da3113f6acce60c630c070ce8
 (curl 8.21.0), which factored the PSL check into a new is_public_suffix()
 helper. In 8.14.1 the check is still inline in Curl_cookie_add(); the fix is
 minimal: after computing dlen and clen, decrement each when the raw input
 ends with '.', then pass the shortened length to Curl_strntolower() and
 explicitly NUL-terminate the buffers (the original code copied dlen+1 /
 clen+1 to include the NUL, which no longer works when we've dropped the
 trailing dot from the count). co->domain is not mutated because it is used
 later for cookiehash() and equality checks.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/51beed175dbfc37da3113f6acce60c630c070ce8
Bug: https://curl.se/docs/CVE-2026-8924.html
Bug-Debian: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-8924
Forwarded: not-needed
Author: Root.io Novel Patcher <security@root.io>
Last-Update: 2026-07-27
---
--- a/lib/cookie.c
+++ b/lib/cookie.c
@@ -907,12 +907,25 @@
     char lcookie[256];
     size_t dlen = strlen(domain);
     size_t clen = strlen(co->domain);
+    /* CVE-2026-8924: strip an optional trailing '.' (RFC 1034 root indicator)
+       from both the request host and the Set-Cookie Domain attribute before
+       delegating to libpsl. libpsl compares byte-for-byte, so "co.uk." would
+       not match the "co.uk" public-suffix entry and a hostile origin could
+       set a cross-domain super-cookie. Only the local lowercased copies are
+       modified; co->domain is left intact because it is used later for
+       cookiehash() and equality checks. */
+    if(dlen && (domain[dlen - 1] == '.'))
+      dlen--;
+    if(clen && (co->domain[clen - 1] == '.'))
+      clen--;
     if((dlen < sizeof(lcase)) && (clen < sizeof(lcookie))) {
       const psl_ctx_t *psl = Curl_psl_use(data);
       if(psl) {
         /* the PSL check requires lowercase domain name and pattern */
-        Curl_strntolower(lcase, domain, dlen + 1);
-        Curl_strntolower(lcookie, co->domain, clen + 1);
+        Curl_strntolower(lcase, domain, dlen);
+        lcase[dlen] = 0;
+        Curl_strntolower(lcookie, co->domain, clen);
+        lcookie[clen] = 0;
         acceptable = psl_is_cookie_domain_acceptable(psl, lcase, lcookie);
         Curl_psl_release(data);
       }
Description: CVE-2026-8926 - netrc credential leak on unmatched login
 When curl is instructed to use a .netrc file for credentials while the URL
 specifies a username without a password (e.g. https://user@host/), the old
 state-machine based parser in lib/netrc.c (parsenetrc) sets
 retcode = NETRC_OK as soon as a machine block matches the host, and
 unconditionally stores the block's password into a local buffer. If the
 requested login never matches during the scan (either because that match
 was the last entry, or because processing terminates at EOF while still
 holding a partially-collected credential), the function returns NETRC_OK
 and leaks the last matched machine's password to the caller as if it
 belonged to the requested user.
 .
 Upstream fixed this in curl 8.21.0 (commit 4ae1d7cc) as part of a full
 scanner refactor that relies on the new lib/creds abstraction, which does
 not exist in 8.14.1. This patch re-implements the security fix as a
 minimal, targeted change against 8.14.1's existing parsenetrc state
 machine: at the parser exit label we downgrade the return code to
 NETRC_NO_MATCH whenever a specific login was requested but never matched
 any of the traversed machine entries. This preserves the
 Curl_parsenetrc(store, host, char **loginp, char **passwordp, filename)
 signature and the data->state.creds_from bitfield model used by url.c and
 by unit1304, so no other files need to change.
 .
 The unit1304 regression test is updated: cases that previously asserted
 result==0 with a NULL password (an artifact of the vulnerable behaviour)
 are corrected to assert result==1 (NETRC_NO_MATCH), and a dedicated
 CVE-2026-8926 case is added that scans "curl.example.com" (the LAST
 machine entry in tests/data/test1304) with a login that does not match
 and asserts no password is returned.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/curl/curl/commit/4ae1d7cc2643e4773a136395f12bc02fc6867854
Bug: https://curl.se/docs/CVE-2026-8926.html
Bug-Debian: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-8926
Forwarded: not-needed
Author: Debian curl maintainers
Last-Update: 2026-07-08
---
--- a/lib/netrc.c
+++ b/lib/netrc.c
@@ -338,6 +338,17 @@
 
 out:
   curlx_dyn_free(&token);
+  /*
+   * CVE-2026-8926: if a specific login was requested but never matched
+   * any of the machine blocks we traversed, do not report success.
+   * Otherwise a password collected from the last matched machine block
+   * (which is stored eagerly by the state machine before it knows
+   * whether the login will ever match) would leak to the caller as if
+   * it belonged to the requested login.
+   */
+  if(!retcode && specific_login && !our_login)
+    retcode = NETRC_NO_MATCH;
+
   if(!retcode) {
     if(!password && our_login) {
       /* success without a password, set a blank one */
